In article <voadncb8svxZHNzYnZ2dnUVZ_vGdnZ2d@comcast.com>, glen herrmannsfeldt <gah@ugcs.caltech.edu> writes:
> 
> The question, than, is can 11/780 OpenVMS run on a 780 with those
> instructions?  Maybe not.

   11/780 shipped with all the instructions standard except the G and
   H float instructions, which were optional.  Later on vector
   instructions were added to the architecture, but VMS does not require
   them to boot, and will emulate them when not found.  The emulation of 
   unimplemented instrutions is in an exception handler that is probably 
   not model dependent (emulation of the vector instructions is
   optionally loadable), models which implement those instructions just 
   don't raise that exception.

   Rule of thumb: VMS (Open or not) is not built in a model-dependent
   manner.  Older versions of VMS will not recognise and therefor not
   support newer systems.  Older versions of VMS may not have any
   support for later I/O hardware.  Newer versions of VMS run on
   anything that has enough disk space except the 11/782, but testing
   and formal support for older models gets dropped.  VMS Engineering
   doesn't have the time to remove the code that makes VMS run on
   older I/O busses et. al.  You'll find few exceptions to this rule.



