Carl, we got an XP1000 from engineering about two years ago.  When it 
arrived (shrink-wrapped on a palette with a bunch of other stuff), it 
was DOA.  In general the same symptoms, mash the power button behind the 
door and NOTHING.  I tried EVERYTHING.  I swapped all the power cords 
coming into the box and got nowhere.  Just in case I missed something, I 
  took the system apart down to the chassis, fastidiously cleaned 
everything, reseated all the components and started with a totally bare 
system.  I finally managed to get it fired up in a minimal configuration 
and added giblets back into the box.  Moved to my desk, powered it up 
for some burn-in time and when I had to move the power source it crapped 
out again with the same symptoms.

Then we moved it to our lab in Alpharetta and let it sit for a couple 
months.  On a whim I powered it up and it worked.  Since then it's been 
power cycled (both by switch and loss of room power) and it's come back up.

There are only two possible things I can identify.  First, that one/some 
of the connectors in the wiring harness for the power supply were not 
making good contact.  I reseated the wiring harnesses to the CPU itself 
several times quite agressively.  There was also the possibility that 
one of the mounting screws for the CPU had fallen behind the CPU and was 
making contact between the motherboard and the chassis and my "rebuild" 
of the system dislodged that screw.  Certainly none of the internal 
peripherals seem to have caused enough grief to drag down the power 
supply and prevent operation.

Another thing to check...  When you have disconnected or powered off the 
system and are ready to plug in the power supply, pay attention to the 
fan.  It shouldn't actually start rotating but you should see it "bump" 
when you plug in the power and naturally turn when you power the system 
on.  The switch might also be bad.

bob



