All,

In the continuing saga of one man's VAX 6660....

To quickly recap: when powered up, the machine's front panel lights up 
for about five seconds, then goes dark; the fans continue to run.  The 
H7206B shows an error LED claiming there's an XMI-1 card in the XMI-2 
backplane; examination shows this is not true, and the same error shows 
even with NO cards in the XMI backplane.  The H7214 and H7242 regulators 
show DC OK for that five seconds, then go dark.  The H7215 never shows 
DC OK.  This machine also has VAXBI, and that side's regulators show DC 
OK; I swapped the H7215s between XMI and VAXBI, and either H7215 works 
on the BI side, but not the XMI side.

The last thing I was asked was: does the XMI-side H7215 show ANY 
activity, i.e. hook up a scope to its output and see if it ever 
flickers.  I did this, and the answer is yes: there is a very brief 
output voltage (I tried this on three different output lines).

I've replaced the H7206B AND the control/status cable between the H7206B 
and the XMI regulators AND the XTC sequencer.  I've compared voltages at 
the various pins of the control connector on the respective H7215s and, 
while they differ, I do not have any information that tells me which is 
which.

So I'm still hoping to find someone who has deeper knowledge of the 
power system and is willing to share.  I really wish I could find 
someone with schematics of the H7206B and XTC sequencer, as it's 
plausible that the replacements don't work, either.  In fact, the H7206B 
has an odd symptom: its internal fan does not turn.  I had intended to 
replace it but, for some reason, decided to test the existing fan before 
I did: it worked fine on a bench supply.  It looks like it feeds off the 
same 24v as the big cabinet fans (which I've verified are really powered 
and turning).  This symptom exists in both the original and the 
replacement.

Does anyone perhaps have any additional service information in an old 
box of fiche?  (I have all the usual "owner's documentation," such as 
the technical information manual, maintenance guide, etc.)  Schematics, 
or at least logic diagrams, would sure help....

Thanks in advance for any light you can shed on the saga of my large 
room fan....  -- Ian

iking (at-thing) killthewabbit (dot-thing) org


