headley sappleton wrote:

> I received two (2) LK250 keyboards. Problem is they come with different 
> cables. lk250 (P/N: 00-lk250-AA)  has a 5-pin DIN cable, like the old PC 
> keyboard cable. The other lk250 (P/N: 70-27433-0A) has a Mini-DIN cable 
> (like a PS/2). Maybe they are differentiated by the Part numbers above. I 
> would like to know whether or not these are legit connectors or someone 
> mistakenly put the wrong cable into one. I any case I would also like to 
> know what hardware/software  both are compatible with.

   The LK250 was the keyboard for the VAXmate and a few similar boxes.  Based on 
what I've just discovered, that keyboard had three major variants, -AA, -PA and 
-TA -- and the usual small herds of language variants -- and there seem to have 
been cable kits.

   LK250-AA was the US English PS/2 cable.
   -A* were language variants.

   LK250-CA was the WPS PS/2.
   -C* were the language variants.

   LK250-D* was a 9-pin D connector.  (I've not seen that keyboard connector.)

   LK250-PA was the US English 6-pin mini DIN cable.  PC/AT, IIRC.
   -P* were language variants.

   LK250-TA was the US 5-pin Mini DIN.  PC/XT, IIRC.

   There were other language variants.

   Based on a few external searches, there was also apparently an LK25E around, 
a programmable keyboard I've not seen nor previously heard about.

   The LK450 was the replacement.

   The 70-class number is a component assembly part number and was not usually a 
salable part.  70-class assemblies were certainly sometimes available a spare, 
yes, but were not usually separately available.  17-class was cables, etc.

   I don't immediately see the translation of the 70-class part number you 
cited, but I haven't particularly dug around for it.  (And there were different 
connectors...)

   Been meaning to post out a collected list of old keyboards (somewhere), and 
I'll add this new little bundle of connector variations onto that backlog.


